"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"][20]={"school_physics_en:M20-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M20-L1 – Wavelength, Frequency and Propagation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M20 – Waves, interference and optics<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>A tagged point on a string moves up and down, but a crest travels to\nthe right. Which speed describes the traveling pattern?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>wave<\/strong> is a traveling disturbance. In a mechanical\nwave, parts of a medium oscillate about equilibrium while the\ndisturbance carries energy and momentum; the particles need not migrate\nwith the pattern. The spatial separation between points in the same\nphase, such as adjacent crests, is the <strong>wavelength<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>λ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin metres. The time between corresponding phases at one fixed position\nis the <strong>period<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin seconds; <strong>frequency<\/strong> is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=1/T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin hertz.<\/p>\n<p>In a uniform, nondispersive medium,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=\\frac{\\lambda}{T}=\\lambda f.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>This follows because a harmonic wave travels one wavelength during\none period. An ideal wave traveling right may be written\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>φ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y(x,t)=A\\sin(2\\pi x/\\lambda-2\\pi ft+\\varphi_0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA <strong>snapshot<\/strong> plots displacement against position; a\n<strong>time trace<\/strong> plots displacement at one position against\ntime. Crucially,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>v<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the speed of the pattern, not the instantaneous speed of an\nindividual string particle. In a dispersive medium phase speed depends\non frequency.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_119_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Adjacent spatial and time graphs of a sinusoidal wave with wavelength and period marked.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Adjacent spatial and time graphs of a\nsinusoidal wave with wavelength and period marked.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Wavelength measures a spatial interval and period a temporal\ninterval.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>Adjacent crests are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.5\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\napart and a fixed string segment oscillates\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>2.0<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntimes each second. Then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=1.5\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=2.0\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=0.50\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=\\lambda f=3.0\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThat string segment does not travel steadily rightward at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3.0\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p>Draw two wave snapshots with the same wavelength but a\nquarter-wavelength phase offset. Distinguish spatial distance, time\ninterval and propagation direction.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How do wavelength and period differ?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A wave has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.80<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=0.80\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=5.0\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCalculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>v<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A source produces 40 cycles per second. Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Does an ideal traveling string wave carry the string particles\npermanently toward the receiver?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Wavelength is a spatial separation; period is a time interval.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.80<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>5.0<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=0.80\\times5.0=4.0\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>40<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.025<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=1/40=0.025\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No. Particles oscillate about equilibrium as the disturbance\ntravels.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The sinusoidal function assumes a harmonic plane wave. Interfaces,\nattenuation and dispersion complicate the constant-speed picture.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M19&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M19-L6<\/a> · Review\nprerequisite<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M20&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M20-L2<\/a> · Next lesson<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2020/2020_06_18-BildungsstandardsAHR_Physik.pdf\">KMK\n– Physics standards for Abitur 2020<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · p. 21, §2.6.2: waves,\npolarisation, double slit, standing waves general/advanced; single slit\nand interferometer advanced · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/mechanik/mechanische-wellen/grundwissen/groessen-zur-beschreibung-einer-welle\">LEIFIphysik\n– Wave quantities<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Basics: wavelength, period and propagation speed\n· Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence<\/h2>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M20-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M20-L2 – Longitudinal and Transverse Waves<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M20 – Waves, interference and optics<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>Why is a wave on a string drawn as a wavy line while sound in air is\noften shown as dense and sparse regions?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p>To classify a mechanical wave compare the <strong>direction of\npropagation<\/strong> of the disturbance with the <strong>direction of\nparticle oscillation<\/strong>. In a <strong>transverse wave<\/strong>,\nmotion is perpendicular to propagation, as on a taut string. In a\n<strong>longitudinal wave<\/strong>, motion is parallel to propagation;\nsound in air travels through moving compressions and rarefactions. In\neither case, particles remain on average near equilibrium within the\nideal model.<\/p>\n<p>A sinusoidal graph of acoustic pressure versus position is\n<strong>not a photograph of air flowing along a zigzag path<\/strong>. It\nis a plot of a measured quantity. Solids can support both longitudinal\nand transverse waves; waves on a water surface often combine multiple\ndirections of motion. One cannot always infer particle paths from the\nvisible shape of the surface alone. Electromagnetic plane waves in a\nvacuum are transverse: their electric and magnetic fields oscillate\nperpendicular to propagation.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_120_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Particle markers oscillate perpendicular to propagation in a string and parallel in an air-wave model.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Particle markers oscillate perpendicular\nto propagation in a string and parallel in an air-wave\nmodel.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>The comparison distinguishes particle oscillation from wave\npropagation.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>A longitudinal sound wave with speed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>340<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=340\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand frequency\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>340<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=340\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=v/f=1.0\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAdjacent pressure maxima are one metre apart in this ideal model; that\nis not the distance traveled by one air molecule in a cycle.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p>Draw two rows of particle markers. Use small arrows perpendicular to\na large propagation arrow for one row, and parallel for the other. Mark\ntwo compression zones in the longitudinal row.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How are particle oscillation and propagation oriented in a\nlongitudinal wave?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Can a solid support a transverse wave?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>λ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>300<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=300\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>150<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=150\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is a sinusoidal pressure plot not a photograph of particle\ntrajectories?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">They are parallel.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Yes, for example as a shear wave.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>300<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>150<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=300/150=2.0\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It displays a measured quantity against time or position, not the\nactual trajectory.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The distinction refers to ideal wave modes. Surface waves,\nanisotropic media and coupled modes can have more complicated particle\nmotion.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M20&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M20-L1<\/a> · Review\nprerequisite<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M20&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M20-L3<\/a> · Next lesson<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2020/2020_06_18-BildungsstandardsAHR_Physik.pdf\">KMK\n– Physics standards for Abitur 2020<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · p. 21, §2.6.2: waves,\npolarisation, double slit, standing waves general/advanced; single slit\nand interferometer advanced · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/mechanik/mechanische-wellen/grundwissen/groessen-zur-beschreibung-einer-welle\">LEIFIphysik\n– Wave quantities<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Basics: wavelength, period and propagation speed\n· Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence<\/h2>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M20-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M20-L3 – Polarisation and Electromagnetic Waves<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M20 – Waves, interference and optics<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>Why does rotating a polarising sheet in front of a display change its\nbrightness?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p>An electromagnetic plane wave in a vacuum consists of changing\nelectric and magnetic fields. The directions of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>E<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>B<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the propagation vector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>k<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">→<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\vec k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare mutually perpendicular. For light, <strong>linear\npolarisation<\/strong> means that the electric-field oscillation has a\nfixed direction transverse to propagation. No material particles are\ntransported along a wavy line.<\/p>\n<p>An ideal linear <strong>polariser<\/strong> transmits the field\ncomponent parallel to its transmission axis. For light that is already\nlinearly polarised, <strong>Malus’s law<\/strong> gives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>α<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=I_0\\cos^2\\alpha,<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>where\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the angle from the incident polarisation direction to the filter\naxis. Parallel axes give maximal ideal transmission; crossed axes give\nzero. For initially <strong>unpolarised<\/strong> light, average\ntransmitted intensity after the first ideal polariser is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_0/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOnly then may the angle-dependent rule be applied to a second filter.\nReal filters also absorb and scatter light. Polarisation is\ncharacteristic of transverse waves.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_121_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Various electric-field orientations before a vertical filter and vertically aligned output components.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Various electric-field orientations\nbefore a vertical filter and vertically aligned output\ncomponents.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>An ideal linear polariser transmits the electric-field component\naligned to its axis.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>After a first filter, an initially unpolarised beam has ideal\nintensity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_1=8.0\\,\\mathrm{W/m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA second filter is turned by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>60<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">60^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mn>60<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8.0<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_2=I_1\\cos^2 60^\\circ=8.0\\times0.25=2.0\\,\\mathrm{W/m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBefore the first filter, the initial unpolarised intensity would ideally\nhave been\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>16<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">16\\,\\mathrm{W/m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p>Use two polarising sheets or a documented simulation with an ordinary\nlight source. Turn one sheet and compare parallel,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>45<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">45^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand crossed settings. Do not view the Sun or improvise laser\nexperiments.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which field component defines the linear polarisation of light?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I/I_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>45<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">45^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin Malus's model?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What ideal intensity passes crossed filters after the first\nfilter?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Can one apply a single fixed angle in Malus’s law to initially\nunpolarised light before the first polariser?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The oscillation direction of the electric field.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mn>45<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos^2 45^\\circ=1/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Zero.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No. Average over incident polarisations first; the ideal mean\nintensity is halved.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>Field arrows show the instantaneous orientation rather than material\ntrajectories. Actual polarisers and non-plane waves deviate from the\nidealisation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M20&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M20-L2<\/a> · Review\nprerequisite<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M20&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M20-L4<\/a> · Next lesson<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2020/2020_06_18-BildungsstandardsAHR_Physik.pdf\">KMK\n– Physics standards for Abitur 2020<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · p. 21, §2.6.2: waves,\npolarisation, double slit, standing waves general/advanced; single slit\nand interferometer advanced · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/optik/polarisation/grundwissen/polarisation-von-licht-einfuehrung\">LEIFIphysik\n– Introduction to light polarisation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Basics: transverse\nelectric-field orientation and filter axis · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/optik/polarisation/grundwissen/polarisation-von-licht-fortfuehrung\">LEIFIphysik\n– Polarisation of light: continuation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Basics: intensity after one\nor two filters · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence<\/h2>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M20-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M20-L4 – Superposition and Interference<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M20 – Waves, interference and optics<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>Why can two oscillations add to a large response at one location but\nnearly cancel at another?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p>The <strong>superposition principle<\/strong> states that in a linear\nsystem displacements or field amplitudes add point by point:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_\\mathrm{total}=y_1+y_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTwo coherent waves of equal frequency and fixed phase relationship can\nform a stable interference pattern. <strong>Path difference<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the difference between path lengths. For sources initially in phase,\nthe related phase difference is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>φ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\varphi=2\\pi\\Delta s/\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta s=m\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(integer\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>),\nwave contributions arrive in phase: <strong>constructive\ninterference<\/strong>. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta s=(m+\\tfrac12)\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthey arrive opposite in phase: <strong>destructive\ninterference<\/strong>. Complete cancellation at a point requires equal\namplitudes, appropriate polarisation and coherence. For light,\nelectric-field amplitudes add, while measured intensity is proportional\nto the time average of the <em>square<\/em> of the summed field.\nTherefore coherent intensities cannot simply be added: two identical\nfields yield four times one beam’s intensity at a constructive maximum.\nWith incoherent sources, stable cross terms average out.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_122_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two plots compare adding in-phase and opposite-phase sinusoids of equal amplitude.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two plots compare adding in-phase and\nopposite-phase sinusoids of equal amplitude.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>In-phase waves reinforce; equal opposite-phase amplitudes\ncancel.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>Two coherent water waves initially in phase have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=0.40\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a path difference of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.60<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta s=0.60\\,\\mathrm m=1.5\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe phase difference is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(equivalent to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>π<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmodulo\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>).\nEqual amplitudes produce an ideal minimum at that point.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p>Use a spreadsheet to add two sine curves of the same frequency: first\nin phase, then shifted by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>π<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCompare the total amplitudes and explain why adding intensities is a\ndifferent operation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is added under linear superposition?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What phase difference corresponds to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta s=\\lambda/4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta s=2\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngive a maximum or minimum for initially in-phase sources?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What additional condition permits complete cancellation?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Displacements or field amplitudes, not their intensities\ndirectly.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>φ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\varphi=\\pi/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A maximum.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Equal amplitudes and suitable polarisation/coherence.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>An initial phase offset alters the conditions; strongly nonlinear\nwaves do not obey simple amplitude superposition.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M20&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M20-L3<\/a> · Review\nprerequisite<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M20&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M20-L5<\/a> · Next lesson<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2020/2020_06_18-BildungsstandardsAHR_Physik.pdf\">KMK\n– Physics standards for Abitur 2020<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · p. 21, §2.6.2: waves,\npolarisation, double slit, standing waves general/advanced; single slit\nand interferometer advanced · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/mechanik/mechanische-wellen/grundwissen/interferenz\">LEIFIphysik\n– Interference of mechanical waves<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Basics: path difference, maxima\nand minima · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence<\/h2>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M20-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M20-L5 – Double-Slit Interference Patterns<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M20 – Waves, interference and optics<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>A double slit forms alternating bright and dark bands. Why does their\nspacing depend on the colour of light?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p>In a <strong>double-slit<\/strong> arrangement, light from two narrow,\ncoherently illuminated openings overlaps. Let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbe the separation between slit centres,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe screen distance and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe transverse screen coordinate. For approximately parallel paths, the\npath difference toward direction\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>θ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta s=d\\sin\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBright fringes of order\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>±<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>±<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=0,\\pm1,\\pm2,\\ldots<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\noccur when<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d\\sin\\theta=m\\lambda.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For small angles with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>≪<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|y|\\ll L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">tan<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin\\theta\\approx\\tan\\theta\\approx y/L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ngiving<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>d<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>d<\/mi><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_m\\approx\\frac{m\\lambda L}{d},\\qquad \\Delta y\\approx\\frac{\\lambda L}{d}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Longer wavelengths place the same order farther from the central\nmaximum. With <strong>polychromatic light<\/strong>, patterns for several\nwavelengths overlap: their central bright fringes coincide\napproximately, while side fringes may spread into colours. Real slits\nhave finite width; their diffraction envelope limits fringe brightness,\nso the ideal formula does not predict every intensity. Insufficient\ntemporal or spatial coherence also reduces contrast.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_123_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two coherently illuminated slits, schematic paths and five maxima on a screen, with orders labelled.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two coherently illuminated slits,\nschematic paths and five maxima on a screen, with orders\nlabelled.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Wavelength, screen distance and slit spacing determine fringe\nseparation.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.30<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.0<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=0.30\\,\\mathrm{mm}=3.0\\times10^{-4}\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=2.0\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>600<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6.0<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>7<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=600\\,\\mathrm{nm}=6.0\\times10^{-7}\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe fringe spacing is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.0040<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta y\\approx\\lambda L/d=0.0040\\,\\mathrm m=4.0\\,\\mathrm{mm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFirst-order maxima lie approximately four millimetres either side of the\ncentre.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p>Use a simulation with adjustable slit separation and wavelength.\nDouble\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthen restore it and increase wavelength. Record how fringe spacing\nchanges. Perform real laser experiments only with approved school\nequipment and expert supervision.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What condition describes a bright interference fringe?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchange when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis doubled?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the example for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>450<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=450\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is special about the centre with polychromatic\nillumination?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d\\sin\\theta=m\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor integer\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Fringe spacing is halved in the approximation.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>3.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta y\\approx3.0\\,\\mathrm{mm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The zero-order maximum approximately coincides for all\nwavelengths.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The derivation assumes small angles, adequate coherence and\nsufficiently narrow slits. Real intensities also follow a single-slit\ndiffraction envelope.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M20&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M20-L4<\/a> · Review\nprerequisite<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M20&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M20-L6<\/a> · Next lesson<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2020/2020_06_18-BildungsstandardsAHR_Physik.pdf\">KMK\n– Physics standards for Abitur 2020<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · p. 21, §2.6.2: waves,\npolarisation, double slit, standing waves general/advanced; single slit\nand interferometer advanced · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/optik/beugung-und-interferenz/grundwissen/doppelspalt\">LEIFIphysik\n– Double slit<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Basics: fringe locations and slit separation · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/3-1-youngs-double-slit-interference\">OpenStax\n– University Physics 3, §3.1 Young’s Double-Slit Interference<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\n§3.1: double slit and small-angle approximation · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence<\/h2>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M20-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M20-L6 – Standing Waves: Nodes and Antinodes<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M20 – Waves, interference and optics<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>Why can some places on a taut string remain nearly still while others\noscillate strongly?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p>Two counter-propagating waves of the same frequency and appropriate\namplitude can superpose to form a <strong>standing wave<\/strong>. At a\n<strong>node<\/strong>, displacement remains zero; at an\n<strong>antinode<\/strong>, the maximum magnitude of displacement is\nlargest. For an ideal string fixed at both ends, both ends must be\nnodes. The allowed standing modes satisfy an integer number of\nhalf-wavelengths fitting into length\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mfrac><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>n<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=n\\frac{\\lambda_n}{2},\\quad \\lambda_n=\\frac{2L}{n},\\quad f_n=\\frac{nv}{2L}\\quad(n=1,2,3,\\ldots).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The fundamental mode,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhas one antinode. Mode\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nadds a node at the midpoint and has two antinodes: it is the first\novertone. Mode\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n+1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnodes including endpoints and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nantinodes. An ideal standing-wave displacement is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>A<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y(x,t)=2A\\sin(n\\pi x/L)\\cos(2\\pi f_nt)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAdjacent antinodes oscillate in opposite directions. A standing wave\ndoes not display a traveling wave shape and, in the ideal lossless case,\ntransports no time-averaged net energy along the string.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_124_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two opposite temporal phases on a fixed string with three nodes and two antinodes.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two opposite temporal phases on a fixed\nstring with three nodes and two antinodes.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>The second mode of a fixed string has three nodes and two\nantinodes.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>For length\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=1.20\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand wave speed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>120<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=120\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe fundamental frequency is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_1=v/(2L)=50\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_2=100\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_2=2L/2=1.20\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThree nodes occur at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.60<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.60<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.20\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p>Draw the first three modes of a string fixed at both ends. Mark nodes\nand antinodes; label the number of half-wavelengths in each mode. Prefer\na simulation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How many antinodes does the third normal mode have?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_1=50\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=1.20\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is an antinode not a crest traveling steadily rightward?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Three.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>150<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_3=3f_1=150\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_1=2L=2.40\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Its location is fixed; only its displacement changes with time.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>These formulas assume a uniform ideal string with fixed ends and\nconstant wave speed. Open-ended air columns have different boundary\nconditions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M20&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M20-L5<\/a> · Review\nprerequisite<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M20&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M20-L7<\/a> · Next lesson<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2020/2020_06_18-BildungsstandardsAHR_Physik.pdf\">KMK\n– Physics standards for Abitur 2020<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · p. 21, §2.6.2: waves,\npolarisation, double slit, standing waves general/advanced; single slit\nand interferometer advanced · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.leifiphysik.de/mechanik/mechanische-wellen/grundwissen/stehende-wellen-bedingungen\">LEIFIphysik\n– Standing waves: boundary conditions<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Basics: nodes, antinodes,\nnormal-mode frequencies with fixed ends · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence<\/h2>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M20-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M20-L7 – Advanced Level: Single Slit and Interferometer<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M20 – Waves, interference and optics<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>Even one narrow slit makes light and dark zones. How can a similar\ninterference effect reveal tiny mirror motions?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Advanced-level extension (eA):<\/strong> A <strong>single\nslit<\/strong> of width\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndiffracts light because many secondary waves inside the aperture\ninterfere. For monochromatic incident light and ideal far-field\n(Fraunhofer) geometry, intensity minima obey<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>±<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>±<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\sin\\theta_m=m\\lambda,\\qquad m=\\pm1,\\pm2,\\ldots.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The central bright region is roughly twice as wide as the spacing\nbetween adjacent minima on one side. For small angles, the first minimum\nlies at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_1\\approx L\\lambda/a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom the centre. Unlike the simplified double slit, the single-slit\npattern shows a broad central maximum and much weaker side maxima.<\/p>\n<p>A <strong>Michelson interferometer<\/strong> uses a beam splitter to\nseparate coherent light into two perpendicular arms. After reflection\nfrom two mirrors, the beams are recombined. Moving one mirror by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchanges its beam’s round-trip optical path by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\Delta x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOne full fringe-period shift is produced approximately when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\Delta x=\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor vacuum light and an otherwise fixed arrangement. An interferometer\ncan therefore detect tiny changes in optical path. High-precision work\nrequires stable optics, well-defined coherence and accounting for phase\nchanges at optical interfaces.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_125_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Single-slit intensity with broad central maximum beside a simplified Michelson setup.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Single-slit intensity with broad central\nmaximum beside a simplified Michelson setup.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Single-slit diffraction has a broad central maximum; the\ninterferometer turns optical-path changes into fringe shifts.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>A single slit of width\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=0.20\\,\\mathrm{mm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis illuminated at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>500<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=500\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nscreen distance is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=2.0\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.0025<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin\\theta_1=\\lambda/a=0.0025<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_1\\approx L\\lambda/a=5.0\\,\\mathrm{mm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe central region extends ideally from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>5.0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-5.0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mn>5.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+5.0\\,\\mathrm{mm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na width near\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10\\,\\mathrm{mm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>500<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=500\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na Michelson mirror displacement of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>250<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">250\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwould move the pattern by one fringe period.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p>Use a simulation to compare single-slit patterns at two slit widths.\nExplain why narrowing a slit broadens the main maximum. Examine\ninterferometers only with a paper diagram or simulation, not an\nimprovised exposed-laser setup.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which condition defines single-slit intensity minima?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What happens to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen slit width is halved?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How far must a Michelson mirror move for one fringe period at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>600<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=600\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is the double-slit condition for maxima not interchangeable with\nthe single-slit minima formula?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>θ<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\sin\\theta_m=m\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor nonzero integer\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Distance to the first minimum doubles in the approximation.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>300<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta x=\\lambda/2=300\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A single slit involves many secondary waves within one opening; the\nconditions are different.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The minima relation assumes monochromatic light, an ideal slit and\nfar-field conditions. Real interferometers require careful optical-path\nand phase accounting.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M20&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M20-L6<\/a> · Review\nprerequisite <strong>Looking ahead:<\/strong> The next module begins with\n<a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M21&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M21-L1 – Photon Energy and the\nExternal Photoelectric Effect<\/a>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2020/2020_06_18-BildungsstandardsAHR_Physik.pdf\">KMK\n– Physics standards for Abitur 2020<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · p. 21, §2.6.2: waves,\npolarisation, double slit, standing waves general/advanced; single slit\nand interferometer advanced · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/4-1-single-slit-diffraction\">OpenStax\n– University Physics 3, §4.1 Single-Slit Diffraction<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §4.1:\nsingle-slit minima and broad central maximum · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/3-5-the-michelson-interferometer\">OpenStax\n– University Physics 3, §3.5 Michelson Interferometer<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §3.5:\nround-trip path length and mirror displacement · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence<\/h2>\n<\/article>"};
